n these two figures  where left was with no antibodies added  and in the b figure the antibodies were  added at the same time of il1 beta  stimulation  and there was no change in the frequency  of the phosphor nf kappa b positive cell  here is our experimental setup that we  used for our  studies we used cryopreserve pbmcs from  healthy donors that were thought  rested and stimulated with il1 beta in  the presence of the tfa labial  antibodies  cells were then fixed with pfa and  barcoded  before stained with the cell surface or  other intracellular antibodies  samples were run inside of and acquired  data was then analyzed using the r-based  computational algorithms side of bank  and by traditional gating  to identify the immune cell subtypes  that respond to il-1 beta stimulation we  first performed spade analysis in  cytobank  state is an algorithm to analyze  multi-parameter data and the way it  works is it basically like puts the  phenotypically similar cells in a  cluster  and then generates a 2d tree of those  clusters  figure a shown over here shows the  localization of the different immune  cell subsets  such as cd4 t cell cd19 t  cell cd8 t cells and so on  the size of each circle and color in  this particular figure depicts the  number of the cells  so blue color depicts less cell count  and red color shown over here depicts  a higher cell count the clusters were  defined  as cd4 t cell cd8 t cells b  cells or monocytes and so on based on  the median expression of the lineage  markers  as shown in figure b the color here  blue to red represents low to high  marker expression  uh figure c shows expression of phospho  in escapa b  phosphop38 and phosphoerc across all  immune cell subtypes  in vehicle treated which is the top  panel and the il1 beta stimulated  samples which is the bottom panel  here the treatment was this figure is  coming from the cell samples that were  treated with il1 beta for 15 minutes  as you can see il1 beta induced  nf kappa b activation within the cd4 t  cells cd4 negative cd8  low t cells cd11c  myeloid dendritic cells and to some  extent in the natural killer cells  p38 activation was observed  primarily in the cd14 monocyte cd11c  dendritic cells and to some  lower level in some of the cd4 t cells  as seen  here within the cd4 bubble  as compared to vehicle there was no  change in the erc activation upon il1  beta stimulation  i just want to emphasize one more thing  that the scale over here  for the three different phosphor  proteins are different  so although the color red is there but  the level of earth activation either  basal  or under stimulated conditions was lower  as compared to phosphop38 and phosphons  kappab  to identify um the specific immune cell  subsets  even within like what are the specific  cell subsets within cd4  t cells or within the monocytes we then  performed  umap and leiden clustering we clustered  the t  cells monocytes and nk cells separately  and the cells that we used for our  clustering analysis  are shown as um are shown in the figure  so we  used this particular excellent gated  with this gate as shown in figure d  for t cells and then as shown in figure  e  these two gates we used either the cd56  positive hladr negative cells for the  natural killer cell clustering  or the dr positive cells for monocyte  and dendritic cell clustering  we identified um 12 distinct t cell  clusters  and when we projected the clusters on a  umap which is a model of dimensionality  reduction  that condenses everything down to two  dimensions  you can see that the cd4 t cells shown  here on top of the umap  and the cd8 t cells the red  the clusters 2 10 and 11 at the bottom  of the umap  they were distinctly separated from each  other as anticipated  we then determine the frequency of these  clusters in the subjects which is shown  in figure b  and as anticipated there was  heterogeneity in cluster abundance  between the subjects  phenotype of the t cell clusters was  annotated  based on the expression of the markers  as shown on the heat map  here in figure c where blue to high  color  in the scale depicts low to high  expression  and in addition to the heat map we also  used the violin plots to annotate these  clusters um the violin plots are not  shown on the slide  and as an example we identified cluster  12 as  memory t-rex based on the expression of  cd4 cd25  cd45 ro and the lack of expression of  ccr7 sorry cd127  um having annotated the clusters we  identified robust activation of nf kappa  b in clusters  3 4 8 and 12 in response to il1 beta  these clusters corresponded to ts17  cells t follicular helper cells cd4  negative cd8 low cd 161 high t  cells and memory t and memory t-rex  uh we also found phospho nf sorry  activation of p38  within the same clusters but the level  of activation of p38  was much lower as compared to the nf  kappa b  interestingly we identified that  all of the four subsets that respond to  il1 beta  are effector memory t cells as defined  by the expression of cd45 ro  and lack of expression of ccr7  next we were curious to see  if the expression of il1 receptor 1  which transmits il1 beta signaling  is limited to the cells that respond to  il1 beta  to test this we separated total t cells  into phosphorus nf kappa b negative and  phosphory nf kappa b positive  populations  and then measured expression of il1  receptor 1.  surprisingly we observed a significantly  lower  expression of receptor one on the  phosphor nf kappa b positive cells  as compared to the phospho nf kappa b  negative cells  under in cells that were treated with  either vehicle control  or il1 data this data suggests  a difference in the expression of other  proteins either  cell surface or intracellular that may  regulate  il1 beta signaling response  il1 receptor 2 is another cell surface  protein  that binds il1 beta but it acts as a  decoy receptor in that it doesn't  transmit the  downstream signaling so thus  the ratio of receptor 1 and receptor 2  on a cell may affect the final signaling  outcome  unfortunately we could not detect il-1  receptor 2 in our sight-off analysis  however using the same antibody clone  we were able to measure il1 receptor 2  expression in pvmcs  that were cultured in the presence of  ntcd3  ntcd28 and il2 and you can see over here  in the in the figure on the right hand  side the middle panel  that even some level of il1 receptor 2  expression was seen  in cells that were cultured for 48 hours  but were not stimulated with the  t cell activating factors shown here on  the right most is the fmo control  that we use to gate the il1 receptor  positive population  a deeper look to identify  the unique marker that marks the t cells  that respond to il1 beta  we found that expression of ccr6  is a common feature among the four  clusters that showed signaling  activation  although the expression of ccr6 varied  between the four clusters  to find out if ccr6 expression would  identify the responsive cells  we separated the total t cells into ccr6  negative and ccr6 positive t cells  and then analyzed for the expression of  nf phospho ns kappa b  and phosphop38 um interestingly as shown  in figure  b over here the uh the nf kappa b  positive cells were enriched within the  ccr6 positive t  cells we observed the same with  phosphop38 positive t  cells furthermore up to 60 percent of  the ccr  6 positive cd4 ma sorry  ccr 6 positive t cells  that responded to il1 beta stimulation  also co expressed ccr2 and cd26  as shown here in the umap overlays  following similar steps we identified  the monocyte clusters  annotated them and determined their  abundance in different subjects  we identified nf catholic activation in  the cd14  classical monocyte subsets cluster 1  2 6 and 9 as well as in the cd11c  myeloid dendritic cell which is cluster  eight shown over here  in addition il1 beta also stimulated  nf kappa b um phosphons kappa b  in cluster 12 which was found to be a  linear negative cell that only  expressed cd38  we don't know what that cell type is yet  p38 activation was also observed within  these same subsets  for clustering nk cells we used the  classic broad nk cell markers  uh such as cd56 cd16 and cd161  and also included other markers as shown  over here on figure c  heat map that we found that were  expressed on the  nk cells after clustering  we identified 17 clusters  and found an f kappa b activation  induced by il1 beta  in cluster in clusters 2  3 and 17. interestingly  cluster 17 that showed the most robust  activation of phosphor nf  activation of nf kappa b and p38 was  found to be the one that uniquely  expressed cd25  cluster 2 corresponded to the cd56  dim cd16 dim population and cluster  3 was the cd56 bright cd16 negative  population  this cluster 17 that expresses cd25  we suspect that these cells could be  either some sort of  innate lymphoid cells or activated nk  cells but  further studies are needed to confirm  their identity  we also evaluated indirect signaling  mediated by il1 beta  by stimulating cells for longer time  points as you can see  il-1 beta stimulation led to a transient  early activation of nf kappa b in t cell  subsets shown over here  that peaked at 15 minutes and then was  reduced to basal levels at later time  points  monocytes cd 11c dendritic cells and nk  cell subsets  also showed similar kinetics of nf kappa  b  and p38 activation but it's not shown  over here  in contrast longer stimulation with il1  beta  for one hour or more led to differential  activation of stat  1 stack 3 and stat 5  in subsets of t cells monocytes and nk  cells  shown here is the data for stat 3  activation in t cell subsets which  clearly shows the donor heterogeneity  and the response  as well as the differential activation  in the different t  cell subtypes for detailed information  for people who might be interested in  knowing more about the specific immune  cell subsets and the direct and indirect  signaling response mediated by il1 beta  please refer to our online manuscript in  the bio archive  um so finally as  anticipated we found that anakinra  inhibits il1 beta induced signaling  as shown here for ns kappa b activation  in the ccr6 positive t  cells cd14 positive monocytes  cd11c myeloid dendritic cells and the  cd25 expressing nk cell cluster  we have other ongoing projects in the  lab  that are focused on testing and  developing immune based signatures  for personalized anti-inflammatory  therapy for cvd  and i thought that i'll just share this  small piece of data  that was done in the heart failure  subjects  so similar to the healthy subjects  in the heart failure subjects l1 beta  induced nf kappa b  activation specifically in the th17  cells  as well as in the cd11c myeloid  dendritic cells  the interesting finding here is that  there is remarkable heterogeneity  in response to il1 beta stimulation as  well as to inhibition by anakinra  we anticipate seeing such heterogeneity  in the covid19  infected patients and we believe that  immune phenotyping these subjects  using a similar approach may discover  immune biomarkers for early  identification of covid19 patients  at risk of cytokine storm and those that  might respond to agents that block  cytokine storm  so finally to summarize uh we have  identified the immune cell subsets that  respond to il1 beta  ccr6 expression was found to be as a  marker for identifying the t  cells that respond to il-1 beta  il-1 beta induced ms kappa b activation  more prominently than p-38 in t  cells dendritic cells and nk cells we  did not  find activation of erc or jnk  by il1 beta stimulation in any cell  subtype  and as compared to other immune cell  subsets  b cell seems to be quite unresponsive to  il1 beta stimulation  and we think that the activation of  stats that we observed at later time  points after il-1 data stimulation  is most likely arising as a result of  the production of other downstream  uh pro-inflammatory cytokines such as  il-6 intraoceron gamma or gmcsf  and that work is currently ongoing in  the lab to look more  into it finally for my acknowledgements  um i'm really thankful to my mentor dr  colleen mcnamara  as well as all the wonderful people in  her lab that  makes work really fun and exciting for  the  il-1 beta specific work um shantel mex  gaming  and michael vignesh were really  contributed significantly for that  project i'm really thankful to  my collaborator dr eli zander in the  biomedical engineering department and  his phd student corey williams  for their help with data analysis  and finally i would like to acknowledge  the wonderful flow cytometry core people  at uva  and aha for supporting this work thank  you  and thank you dr kasari for that  informative presentation  and we will now start the q a portion of  the webinar  audience members if you have questions  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  so it looks like dr katharine that we  already have some questions  coming in from our audience members our  first question  what do you think are the reasons for  the variability in the donor response  to il1b  yeah that's a great question and  as we initially we thought that it could  be  the level of the receptor one or the  receptor receptor two present on the  cell surface that will determine  the response in the donor but then like  based on our t  cell data it kind of like suggests that  it is a little bit independent of the  receptor one  and there could be multiple factors for  that one of them  is we think that definitely there will  be  like uh it is genetically encoded  so we know that there is polymorphism in  il1 receptor  as well as the other intracellular  signaling proteins such as  mid-88 and so that could  be one of the really possible reason  that why  subjects show differential response and  then what we have not currently measured  is the level of the other signaling  proteins as well as the core receptors  that uh mediate the eye that mediate the  signaling  so uh in all i think the interaction  between them  dictate the signaling outcomes so the  levels of  the different receptors and the core  receptors  i think also makes a difference in the  signaling outcome  and that's how that impacts the donor  heterogeneity in response to l1 beta  thank you for that response one audience  member wants to know  do you plan to also measure downstream  intracellular signaling proteins  involved in the il  1b pathway yeah of  a do and uh we've been like we have  spent some time  now um trying to do that one and one of  the major  uh problem is that we don't have  currently we don't have the antibodies  like good antibodies that are compatible  for flow or site off um and we are we  have uh kind of like tried to find  different antibodies we have some  that we are currently validating uh but  it is tough i just  hope that the the fluidized people or  other company people like come up with  these  other antibodies that are really  important such as for the il1 beta  pathway or il6 pathway  because that would make our work really  easier and it would provide more  insights a deeper insight into what's  exactly going on so yeah we are looking  forward to it but uh some some work is  ongoing and we don't have  all those antibodies  thank you dr and again thank you to our  audience members  any questions not as answered today will  be answered via email  we have time for a couple more questions  before we close  how did you decide to use umap  as opposed to all the other clustering  all the resins um  available um yeah so  uh that's a good question um in the way  so  we use uh spade and i personally like  spa uh to use spade  because then uh when we are working with  example looking  at intracellular signaling and our  question is like how it  uh impacts the different cell subsets so  spate provides a quick  overview it is to look at  what's going on across different immune  cell cell subsets  and then we can go on into more deeper  other clustering algorithms which are  more complicated but at the same time  they provide more detailed information  and it is also much easier i know i mean  with spade you can still get the  more quantitative data from each node  and other things but it is  i think to me it is a little bit more  time taking as compared to  using other clustering algorithms like  uh leiden or looping  if you have the right people to help you  out with those  analysis thank you so much  one last question is anyone studying the  il-1  beta pathways in covid patients with  adverse  heart impact  so my answer to that question would be i  think it is very much  likely i'm not completely aware of that  one  but uh we know that a lot of the  patients who are getting  severe symptoms after a covid 19  infection  they have these comorbidities and a lot  of them have  cardiac issues so i think that's a valid  reason and  inflammation is a common pathway for the  pathogenesis of both  and we are seeing that in coped 19  infected patients a lot of them are  dying due to  these coagulopathies or heart related  problems  so that's an important area to study and  i believe like somebody is definitely  studying that  sorry thank you again for today's  presentation and  very important research i also want to  thank fluidine  for sponsoring today's event and  encourage audience members to check out  other presentations and explore the  virtual environments and booths  and before we go i want to thank  audience members for joining us today  and for their interesting questions we  did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration  and this webcast can be viewed on demand  through july of 2021  until next time have a great day stay  safe  thank you thank you  you


